nd the knight so this knight does  need to come back to d3 but this is a  tough spot the knight and the bishop are  attacking this pawn on f4  this bishop on c4 is still attacking  this f7 square that is yes defended by  the ninth but you don't just want to  leave your knight on h6 the entire time  so finding the best way for black to  continue is difficult you know if you  try to just push forward on f3 okay well  you could just take here  and take i think is extremely bad  because now you take with your queen on  f3 and now you're attacking with your  queen and your bishop and now you're  really putting a lot of pressure on your  opponent to just stay here with knight  on h6 but if instead if they don't take  well what if they just play bishop to e7  but now we can just again take on bishop  f4 and we've completely controlled the  center of the board and now it's even  easier if we want to to castle on the  queen side  and now how does black really continue i  think this is a good spot for for white  the last variation we're going to look  at is if they play bishop to  g7 now reminder if they're not attacking  our knight we're playing bishop to c4 if  they are attacking our knight we need to  figure out how we're going to respond  now we have a couple options we can go  ahead and take on g4 but i like the  approach of playing  d4  d4 allows us to control the center of  the board we're not under any immediate  threat right now especially not from a  pawn if they attack us with a pawn we  obviously have to move but pawn to d4  opens up we can now play bishop takes on  f4 we can get our light square bishop  bald into the game so i think this is  the best approach now if they play d6 we  have to look at things a little bit  differently before we could always play  knight takes on g4 which we're still  going to do here but they really  wouldn't capture with their bishop  because then we would just take their  bishop and that's not really the best  exchange because typically this tends to  be an open board state meaning there's  usually not a lot of pawns in the center  of the board towards the end game and  bishops are better than knights in that  position just because they can control  more of the board  but in this position it's a little bit  different because once the queen comes  to g4 then black can actually take the  pawn on d4 so it is slightly different  in the exchange on why they may actually  decide to go ahead and take with their  bishop on at g4 but that's completely  okay because now we can just take their  pawn  on f4  and then both sides are  equal in material both sides have six  pawns neither side is feeling great  about their position but white does have  the bishop pair and i think going to the  end game they have a better setup maybe  they play knight to c6 getting material  involved into the game we can do the  same now if they want to exchange i  think that's fine yes we have the double  pawns on the c file which is not great  for us but now they have no bishops we  have the bishop here i think going to  the end game this would be good for us i  don't necessarily see them giving up  their only bishop but they definitely  could if they don't that's fine as well  we can get our star score bishop  involved into the game we could even  castle on the queen side depending on  how they continue uh but all in all this  is how i would approach it so those are  the four most common variations that you  will run into  but thank you guys so much for watching  the video thank you to our sponsor  skillshare remember link in the  description below if you'd like to sign  up for a free month first thousand  people to click on that and sign up  we'll get a free month thanks again to  them  if you have another video recommendation  please let me know in the comments below  i'll definitely be looking at those to  see what you guys want to see in the  future but thanks for watching i'll see  you in the next video  you
 

